{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1795034957,"nonce":"y5+ncTXOyrm9rcyT0a4CxsdhXuU=","target":"af5e85a7d37b1f7b459064108b6797a39ae2b11f45eaa947e2cc8406174b0146","message":"Provide opt-in Haxe knowledge metadata on typed asset and application-data declarations. Metadata describes semantic types, stable identities, selected properties, relationships and spatial meaning so the data can enrich a knowledge graph. Developer-facing annotations must use knowledge/domain terminology, without a project prefix; settle exact spellings with the typed API. The compiler generates descriptors and typed access/update bindings rather than requiring users to construct graph records by hand. Projection is an adapter implementation detail. Normal asset loading, saves and preferences require no graph runtime or service, and content addressing remains an independent opt-in.\n\nScope includes authored lore and narrative facts; game progress such as quests, achievements, discovered knowledge and inventory; NPC generative-behavior context such as memories, observations, goals and social relationships; changing world state; relationships between entities and worlds and between entities; and object spatial data. These are examples of extensible application schemas, not a closed game-only vocabulary. Support both authored/embedded data and explicitly selected runtime values. NPC knowledge can differ from world facts; preserve context/provenance and distinguish authored, observed and generated claims where the schema requires it. Do not automatically treat generated content as canonical world truth.\n\nSeparate identities: logical asset address (stable alias), immutable content ID (algorithm-qualified digest of exact stored bytes), and entity/world-instance ID (including world and instance namespace). A changed asset updates the address-to-content edge; moving an entity does not change the content identity. Hash cooked payload bytes, retain source-to-variant provenance, verify expected digests on load and reject mismatches before caching or projection. Mutable knowledge, player/campaign progress and world/entity records need stable IDs plus scoped revisions, not a new semantic identity whenever their serialized content changes. Referenced immutable assets may still carry content IDs.\n\nKnowledge schema: permit application-defined semantic node types, typed properties and named relationships with compile-time schema validation and stable references. AssetAddress -\u003e RESOLVES_TO -\u003e AssetContent, content dependencies, world membership and entity asset usage remain useful built-in mappings, alongside application facts such as NPC knowledge of a lore item, quest progress and entity relationships. Carry player/campaign/session/world scope as applicable. Store local/world transforms, coordinate-space/world identifiers, bounds, units and frame/revision on the relevant instances rather than immutable asset blobs. Generate typed accessors for selected live fields so changes and relation removals can produce versioned updates. Keep authoritative state and update policy explicit; graph materialization is a rebuildable, versioned view unless the application explicitly chooses another authority model.\n\nLocal KyuGraph evidence: crates/kyu-graph re-exports the public API; Connection::query_with_params binds typed values; Connection::apply_delta accepts DeltaBatchBuilder and atomically commits a batch with idempotent upserts. Use parameter bindings and stable batch/source IDs instead of composing Cypher values into strings. Schema migrations, removal/tombstones and reconnect/replay must be idempotent; bound/coalesce updates, never block rendering or run one transaction per transform per frame.\n\nQueries must combine semantic knowledge and changing state: for example lore known by an NPC, quest progress for a player/campaign, related entities in a world and nearby objects relevant to a behavior decision. Asset identity/dependencies, world membership and instances using a content ID are part of that same model, not its entire purpose. Combine relationships with region/bounds predicates. The inspected KyuGraph code does not establish a dedicated spatial index/query API; validate that capability before claiming efficient geometric queries. Begin with explicit numeric positions/AABBs and a coarse world-space cell relation if supported, then measure/index appropriately. A canvas/world ID and transform revision prevent mixing coordinates from different worlds or stale parent transforms.\n\nPlatform boundaries: the inspected KyuGraph kyu-api currently brings JIT, Arrow Flight/Tokio and storage dependencies. Do not silently embed it in browser/iOS packages or assume its portability. Plan a separate optional adapter and a backend-neutral projection batch/query interface: supported native embedded use, and an application-configured connected endpoint for mobile/browser when embedded support is unavailable. Network/auth/authority policy belongs to the connected application. Include only annotated/application-selected data. Save-backed game progress is eligible when explicitly selected; never mirror all saves, preferences or arbitrary file contents automatically. Storage location does not determine semantic eligibility.\n\nValidation: annotation absent produces no hash/projection work or graph dependency; deterministic IDs across runtimes; duplicate content deduplication while two scene instances retain distinct transforms; cooked variants/provenance; address rebinding and deletion; graph replay/rebuild; moved/parented entities and world changes; query relationships plus bounds; stale/reordered revisions; bounded queue/reconnect; native/mobile/browser adapter contract coverage. Record platform limitations explicitly. Include static lore and mutable progress/world/NPC knowledge in the same conformance examples; ensure semantic IDs survive edits and referenced content changes; validate typed relationship targets and selected-field extraction; handle relation deletion and scoped revisions; exercise compound knowledge/relationship/spatial queries. CRDT synchronization is optional backing for mutable knowledge, not a requirement for authoring or querying metadata.\n\nParent file-access issue: 49624d318b0b2d8efec7e26ccd271c6004e5150e4fe8b5dd33e105ee21c472fb. Parent hxnme plan: 88ad5eff5f418581d790175f13ad89d85d24c50e980dca23033b4d884329b3cd. This knowledge-metadata planning does not replace the M0 bundled-file and persistent-save round-trip requirement.","files":null}]}